-- §5  The two self-joins of the parts, and the group sizes that fix how many rows each returns.
# One row per (relation, group size): which self-join, how it pairs the rows of one group, a group
# size `k`, how many groups have that size, and how many rows the self-join returned.
#
# A self-join's size is not free
#   A self-join on a key pairs the rows that share the key. Grouping composition/parts.sqlc by the
#   join key gives groups of `k` rows, and the number of rows the self-join returns is then fixed by
#   those sizes and by how it pairs: whether it keeps a row paired with itself, and whether it keeps
#   both orders of a pair. composition/sibling_parts.sqlc keeps a row paired with itself on purpose,
#   and eliminations/derived.sqlc keeps each unordered pair once, and both headers say so; this is
#   what makes those statements fail when the join changes.
#
# NULL keys are left out
#   `GROUP BY` puts NULLs in one group and `=` never matches one, so the two counts would disagree
#   for a reason that is not a defect.
#
# A check for drift
#   As algebra/searches.sqlc says of its own union, the count holds for any self-join on an exact
#   key. What it catches is somebody changing the key or the pairing with nothing saying so.
-- composition/parts.sqlc grouped by each self-join's key, beside the self-join itself.
SELECT 'composition/sibling_parts.sqlc' AS "relation!", 'ordered, reflexive kept' AS "pairing!",
       f.k AS "k!", count(*) AS "fibres!",
       (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(composition/sibling_parts.sqlc) ) s) AS "joined!"
FROM ( SELECT count(*) AS k
       FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p
       WHERE p.composition IS NOT NULL AND p.composed_layer IS NOT NULL
         AND p.part_filing IS NOT NULL
       GROUP BY p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing ) f
GROUP BY f.k
UNION ALL
SELECT 'eliminations/derived.sqlc', 'unordered, reflexive dropped',
       f.k, count(*),
       (SELECT count(*) FROM ( :compose(eliminations/derived.sqlc) ) d)
FROM ( SELECT count(*) AS k
       FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p
       WHERE p.part_filing IS NOT NULL AND p.part_layer IS NOT NULL
       GROUP BY p.part_filing, p.part_layer ) f
GROUP BY f.k
ORDER BY 1, 3
